ArcLibre Extras disc
====================

This disc goes with the ArcLibre 2 MB ROM. It holds three parts of RISC OS
3.11 that the 2 MB ROM has no room for:

  DOSFS       the Acorn filing system for PC (MS-DOS FAT) discs, as a
              module loaded from disc
  !Alarm      the Acorn alarm clock and diary
  !Printers   the Acorn printer manager, with its printer drivers, and
              PrintDefs, its printer definitions

The 4 MB ROM, for the A5000, A3010, A3020 and A4000, has all three in ROM
and does not need them from this disc.

It also holds two applications for either ROM, which RISC OS 3.11 users got
on the Applications disc:

  !System     where applications find the modules they share
              (System:Modules), and installers put theirs
  !Scrap      where applications put files they pass to each other

Copy any of them to a hard disc or another floppy to keep them with your own
applications.

!System and !Scrap
------------------

Copy !System and !Scrap to the root directory of your hard disc: they must
be somewhere applications can write, so not on a write-protected floppy.
After you switch on, open the hard disc's directory once, so the Filer sees
them and sets them up. Installers such as Acorn C/C++'s say "System
resources cannot be found" until it has.

If there is more than one !System or !Scrap, the first one the Filer sees is
used; double-click one to use it instead. Each has a !Help file.

DOSFS
-----

From the desktop, double-click DOSFS in this disc's directory viewer (a
Module file runs *RMRun, which loads it). Or press F12 and type

  *RMLoad ADFS::ArcLibre.$.DOSFS

then press Return twice. *Help DOSFS shows the loaded module, and *Help
DOSMap and *Help CopyBoot describe its commands. DOSFS needs about 55K of
module area (50K for the module, 5K for its workspace), plus buffers for
each PC disc in use. *RMKill DOSFS removes it again.

With DOSFS loaded, ADFS recognises PC discs in the floppy drive: 720K
DOS/M discs, 1.44M DOS/Q discs on the A5000's high-density drive, and 360K
DOS/N discs. A file of type DOSDisc (&FC8) holding a PC hard-disc
partition opens as a directory. Open the drive icon as usual, or use paths
such as ADFS::0.$.README/TXT. PC names are at most eight characters plus a
three-character extension; RISC OS shows the dot as "/". With *Configure
Truncate On, longer names are shortened to eight characters; a longer
extension is always an error. Files are typed from their extensions with
*DOSMap, for example

  *DOSMap TXT FFF

Formatting: DOSFS provides the DOS/M, DOS/Q and other formats for *Format
and the drive icon's Format menu, for example

  *Format 0 DOS/M

(answer Y to confirm). The boot sector DOSFS writes when formatting is a
data-disc sector: a PC started from the disc only shows a message asking
for the disc to be removed. DOSFS cannot make PC system (boot) discs;
*CopyBoot copies another disc's PC boot code.

Alarm
-----

Double-click !Alarm. Its clock appears on the icon bar; click Select on it
to set an alarm, and Menu for its options. Double-clicking a saved alarms
file (type Alarms, &AE9) starts Alarm with those alarms once the Filer has
seen !Alarm. Alarm needs 96K of application memory.

Printing
--------

!Printers has the three classes of printer of RISC OS 3.11: dot matrix,
LaserJet and PostScript. The printer driver modules they use are inside
!Printers: PDriver, PDriverDP (dot matrix and LaserJet), PDriverPS
(PostScript), PDumperSupport, PDumperDM (dot matrix) and PDumperLJ
(LaserJet).

Double-click !Printers. Its icon appears on the icon bar. Drag a printer
definition from the PrintDefs directory to that icon to install the
printer: FX-80 (an Epson FX-80 dot matrix printer), LasJet-II (an HP
LaserJet II) or LW (an Apple LaserWriter, a PostScript printer). Click Menu
on the icon to configure it, choose where its output goes (the parallel or
serial port, or a file) and make it the current printer. Applications such
as Draw and Paint then print through it.

This !Printers has no AUN remote printing (its modules are not published).
Nor does it have MakePSFont, the module that maps the desktop's outline
fonts to a PostScript printer's own fonts and downloads them: text in
outline fonts may not print correctly on a PostScript printer.

